<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinmukhamed Kunaev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dinmukhamed Kunaev was a Kazakh Communist leader who headed the Communist Party of the Kazakh SSR from 1960 to 1962 and again from 1964 to 1986, and sat on the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>. A close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a>, he oversaw the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a> and of industry in the republic. His replacement by the Russian Gennady Kolbin in December 1986 set off the Jeltoqsan protests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinmukhamed-kunaev.jpg" alt="Dinmukhamed Kunaev" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kunayev_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
